<header class="page-head">
  <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
  <h1>Biography</h1>
  <p class="lede">A scholar who never left the street</p>
</header>
<div class="prose">
<p>Wayne Martin Mellinger, Ph.D., is an independent sociologist, writer, street outreach worker, and public intellectual based in Santa Barbara, California. His life has moved through universities and homeless encampments, professional success and incarceration, addiction and recovery, scholarship and public service. Rather than separating these experiences, Wayne draws upon all of them in his efforts to understand how institutions treat people — and how lives can be rebuilt through connection, accountability, and care.</p>
<p>Wayne was born in Springfield, Massachusetts, in 1958 and raised across the Connecticut River in West Springfield. His father worked long hours to support the family, first as a Fuller Brush salesman and later managing a camera department. His mother, who had survived polio and used a wheelchair throughout Wayne's childhood, was the energetic matriarch of the family — and watching strangers alternately stare at her or avoid her gaze gave Wayne an early education in disability, difference, and the subtle ways people can be denied ordinary recognition. Books and education were central to the family: his paternal grandfather was a Congregational minister, his maternal grandmother became a teacher after being widowed, and three of Wayne's grandparents held graduate degrees.</p>
<p>His childhood was also marked by emotional turmoil. Sensitive, intellectually curious, and struggling with a severe stutter, Wayne often retreated into a closet with a flashlight, a cat, and an old encyclopedia. He read voraciously but was sometimes placed with slower readers because he could not read aloud fluently, and he was beginning to recognize that he was gay at a time when few young people could speak of it openly. He responded by throwing himself into school — student senate, newspaper, honor societies, chess and swim teams, band — and worked long hours to save for college.</p>
<p>In 1976, Wayne entered the University of Massachusetts Amherst, where affordable public education made his intellectual life possible. UMass exposed him to radical political economy, Marxist scholarship, ecological thought, and the performing arts; dance became one of his first great passions. During his final year he met Rodney Beaulieu, and their relationship would last nineteen years, taking them from Massachusetts to Paris, San Francisco, Santa Barbara, and Ventura.</p>
<p>In 1980, Wayne spent an academic year at the University of Paris–Sorbonne, where he and Rodney lived simply, explored museums, and traveled Europe on very little money — an experience that expanded Wayne's world into social theory, philosophy, and the Frankfurt School. Afterward the couple moved to San Francisco's Haight-Ashbury, living there in the brief window between Harvey Milk's assassination and the devastation of AIDS. It was a period of extraordinary possibility for a young gay man, and Wayne's encounters with feminist thought, gay liberation, radical ecology, and Goddess spirituality led him to begin keeping journals and writing poetry.</p>
<p>In 1983, Wayne entered the doctoral program in sociology at the University of California, Santa Barbara, training under Don H. Zimmerman, Thomas P. Wilson, and Harvey Molotch in a department central to the development of ethnomethodology and conversation analysis — the close empirical study of how people produce social order through everyday interaction. He completed his Ph.D. in 1990 and went on to teach at the Santa Barbara, Santa Cruz, and Berkeley campuses of the University of California, as well as Fielding Graduate University, Ventura College, and Antioch University Santa Barbara, becoming a successful, well-compensated professor and homeowner.</p>
<p>Then his life came apart. The end of his nineteen-year partnership in 1999 precipitated a profound psychological crisis: mania, psychosis, substance use, and homelessness followed in rapid succession, taking his relationship, his home, his career, and his sense of identity with them. He was arrested and incarcerated, and he learned during this period that he was living with bipolar disorder. Wayne does not conceal this history or reduce it to a polished story of triumph — recovery was neither immediate nor linear, and rebuilding required treatment, accountability, community, and the difficult work of learning how to live again.</p>
<p>His experience on the street changed the direction of his scholarship and public life. Homelessness was no longer an abstract social problem; Wayne knew what it meant to lose credibility, to be treated as a case rather than a person, and to navigate systems whose paperwork could determine whether someone received shelter, treatment, or nothing at all. After stabilizing his own life, he began working with people experiencing homelessness, mental illness, and addiction, and for nearly two decades his sociological training has been tested against street-level reality — the empirical foundation for his current theoretical project, Critical Interactionism, which examines abandonment as something organizations actively produce through ordinary practices of classification, documentation, and delay.</p>
<p>Wayne has served on the Santa Barbara County Behavioral Wellness Commission, serves on the county's Continuum of Care, and sits on the boards of CLUE-SB, Showers of Blessing, and the Committee for Social Justice. He co-founded the Santa Barbara Homeless Foot Washing and helped establish the Freedom Warming Centers, and each December 21st he organizes the Longest Night Homeless Memorial Vigil at the Santa Barbara County Courthouse, where the community gathers to remember neighbors who died while homeless. He writes columns for Noozhawk, translating sociological research, political analysis, and street-level experience for a general audience.</p>
<p>Wayne's scholarship extends into two ongoing research platforms. <em>Doing Modernity: Studies in Critical Interactionism</em> houses his work on institutions, recognition, and the Santa Barbara School of ethnomethodology. <em>The Dionysian Naturalist: Studies in Religious Naturalism and Other Emerging Nature Religions</em> develops a parallel line of inquiry into contemporary Paganism, religious naturalism, and ecstatic spiritual practice — a personal theological framework he calls Dionysian Naturalism. He is a practicing member of the Unitarian Society of Santa Barbara, and also writes poetry and literary fiction.</p>
<p>In 2025, Wayne published <a href="https://philarchive.org/rec/MELIJW"><em>I Just Wanted to Dance with Dionysus: Life's Sacred Journey, Lost Souls and Our Thirst for Wholeness</em></a>, a Gonzo autoethnography about addiction, homelessness, psychiatric crisis, and recovery, structured through Joseph Campbell's Hero's Journey and the mythology of Dionysus. The book turns personal experience into sociological evidence — not to romanticize suffering, but to examine the institutions and social worlds through which a damaged life must travel.</p>
<p class="thesis">The thread connecting it all is a refusal to separate scholarship from lived experience — or intellectual rigor from the work of showing up. Every person remains a human being with a history, a voice, and the possibility of another chapter.</p>
</div>
